.TH RUST\-XPRA 1 "2026\-07\-30" "rust\-xpra 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rust\-xpra \- Xpra client written in Rust
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rust\-xpra
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ HOST : PORT " | " URI ]
.br
.B rust\-xpra
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.br
.B rust\-xpra
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rust\-xpra
connects to an
.B xpra
server and displays the windows of the applications running there as
ordinary windows on the local desktop, forwarding keyboard, pointer and
clipboard input back to the server.
.PP
It is built on
.B winit
and
.B softbuffer
rather than GTK or Qt, so it has no desktop toolkit dependency; window contents
are pixels decoded from the server's picture encodings and blitted to the screen.
.PP
This is a proof of concept, and it requires an
.B xpra 6.6
or later server, left in its default backwards\-compatible mode
.RB ( XPRA_BACKWARDS_COMPATIBLE
unset or
.BR 1 ).
There is no server implementation: this program is a client only.
.PP
The client takes at most one argument, the connection target, and has no options
beyond
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
More than one argument, or a target that cannot be parsed, is an error (exit
status 34).
.SH OPTIONS
Options and the connection target may be given in either order. An argument that
begins with
.B \-
and is not one of the options below is an error rather than a target, so a mistyped
option is never taken for a host name.
.PP
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
are honoured wherever they appear on the command line, even alongside arguments that
would otherwise be rejected. Each prints to standard output and exits successfully.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short summary of the usage, the connection targets and the environment
variables below.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version of this client. This is the version of
.B rust\-xpra
itself, and not the version of the
.B xpra
protocol it announces to the server, which follows the xpra release whose protocol
it implements.
.TP
.B \-\-ssl\-insecure
Connect to an
.B ssl://
or
.B wss://
server without verifying its certificate chain or its host name. Certificates are
verified against the system trust store by default; this option turns both checks
off, which is what a server using a self\-signed certificate needs. The connection
is then encrypted but not authenticated, and so open to interception. Using this
option with any other kind of target is an error.
.SH CONNECTION TARGET
The target is either a bare
.IR HOST : PORT ,
which is taken to mean
.BR tcp ,
or a URI in one of the following forms:
.TP
.BI tcp:// HOST : PORT /
Plain, unencrypted TCP.
.TP
.BI ssl:// HOST : PORT /
TCP with TLS. The certificate and host name are verified against the system trust
store unless
.B \-\-ssl\-insecure
is given (see
.B LIMITATIONS
below).
.TP
.BI ws:// HOST : PORT /
The xpra WebSocket transport over plain HTTP.
.TP
.BI wss:// HOST : PORT /
The xpra WebSocket transport over HTTPS. Certificates are verified, and
.B \-\-ssl\-insecure
applies, as with
.BR ssl .
.TP
.BI ssh:// [ USER @] HOST [: PORT ]/[ DISPLAY ]
Tunnel over
.BR ssh (1),
which is run as a subprocess: it executes
.B xpra _proxy
.RI [ DISPLAY ]
on the remote host, which bridges the connection to the socket of an already
running display. This requires
.B ssh
on
.B PATH
locally and
.B xpra
installed remotely.
.IP
Authentication must not need to read from standard input, since that carries the
xpra protocol \- use a key held by an
.BR ssh\-agent (1),
or a passphrase\-less key. Host\-key confirmation and password prompts still work,
because
.B ssh
reads those from the controlling terminal rather than from standard input.
.PP
Any other protocol is rejected. A port is required for every scheme except
.BR ssh ,
which defaults to 22. For
.B ssh
the path component is the xpra display number (for example
.BR ssh://server/100 );
for the other schemes it is the HTTP path the server is published under, which is
usually empty.
.PP
The
.I USER
part of an
.B ssh
target is the remote login name. For the other schemes a user name can only be
given in the connection dialog described below, where it is sent to the server as
part of the session handshake for servers that authenticate per user.
.SH CONNECTION DIALOG
Started with
.B no
argument at all \- which is what the desktop entry does \-
.B rust\-xpra
opens a small dialog asking for the connection details instead of exiting: a
protocol drop\-down (which pre\-fills the port with that protocol's default: 10000,
or 22 for
.BR ssh ),
a host, a port, and an optional user name and password.
.PP
.B Tab
moves between the fields, the arrow keys pick the protocol,
.B Enter
connects and
.B Esc
cancels (exit status 0). Connecting happens in the background, so the dialog stays
responsive and reports a failure \- wrong port, no server listening, a rejected
handshake \- in place, ready for another attempt, rather than exiting.
.PP
The password collected here is the
.I session
password: it answers the server's authentication challenge without prompting
again. It is never the
.B ssh
password, which
.B ssh
itself asks for on the terminal.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
A server that requires a password sends a challenge, which the client answers with
an HMAC\-SHA256 digest of the password. The password is taken from, in order:
.IP 1. 4
the connection dialog's password field, when it was filled in;
.IP 2. 4
the
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
environment variable, if set (non\-interactive, for scripts);
.IP 3. 4
.BR pinentry (1),
if one is found on
.B PATH
\- the same prompt GnuPG uses;
.IP 4. 4
otherwise a small built\-in password dialog, so a prompt is always available.
.PP
Only the
.B hmac+sha256
digest is implemented; it is the only one advertised, so the server always
picks it. Kerberos, GSS, SCRAM, U2F and the legacy
.BR xor / des
digests are not supported and fail cleanly. The digest never reveals the password,
and the server mixes in a fresh per\-connection salt so a captured response cannot
be replayed \- but the session itself is in the clear over
.B tcp
and
.BR ws ,
so use
.BR ssl ,
.B wss
or
.B ssh
where confidentiality matters.
.SH SESSION
Windows appear, move, resize and close as the server tells them to; closing a
window asks the server to close that application window, it does not disconnect
the session. Since there is no system tray icon on Linux (see
.B LIMITATIONS
below), there is no way to quit from the GUI: the client exits when the server
disconnects or shuts down, when the connection is lost, or when the process is
interrupted \- typically with
.B ^C
in the terminal it was started from.
.PP
Picture encodings decoded here are
.BR jpeg ,
.B png
and
.BR webp .
Plain\-text clipboard synchronization is supported. Server\-forwarded notifications
are written to the client's log. A server\-forwarded bell writes the terminal bell
character to standard error, so it is only audible when the client was started
from a terminal whose bell is enabled.
.PP
Log output goes to standard output, coloured by level when that is a terminal.
Once the server confirms that it accepts them, informational and higher messages
are also forwarded to the server's own log, which is useful when the client runs
without a visible terminal.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
The session password, used to answer an authentication challenge without
prompting. See
.B AUTHENTICATION
above.
.TP
.B PINENTRY_PROGRAM
The
.B pinentry
binary to prompt with, tried before
.B pinentry
and
.B pinentry\-mac
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B GPG_TTY
Passed to
.B pinentry
as its terminal name, for the curses variants.
.TP
.B XPRA_MMAP
Shared memory picture transfers, used when the server runs on the same host: the
server writes uncompressed pixels into a file the client maps, instead of encoding
them onto the socket. Enabled by default. Set to
.B no
to switch it off, or to an absolute path to pin the backing file \(em an existing
file is used as it is and never removed, which is how a shared memory device can be
used between a host and a virtual machine.
.TP
.B XPRA_MMAP_DIR
The directory to create the shared memory file in. Defaults to the temporary
directory.
.TP
.B XPRA_MMAP_SIZE
The size of the shared memory area, with an optional
.BR K ", " M " or " G
suffix. Defaults to 128M; the server rejects anything below 64M.
.TP
.BR USER ", " USERNAME
The user name sent to the server in the session handshake, when none was given in
the connection dialog.
.TP
.B HOSTNAME
The client host name reported to the server.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, log output is never coloured, whether or not standard output is a
terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The values match those of xpra's own client, so wrapper scripts can treat both the
same way:
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit: the server said goodbye, or the connection dialog was cancelled.
.TP
.B 1
The connection was lost after the session had started.
.TP
.B 7
The server disconnected an established session with an error.
.TP
.B 8
The
.B ssh
transport could not be set up.
.TP
.B 9
An unusable packet was received from an established session.
.TP
.B 14
An internal error, such as a failure to create a window.
.TP
.B 16
The TLS handshake failed.
.TP
.B 18
No session was ever established: the connection was refused or unreachable, the
peer is not an xpra server, or the server rejected the client during the
handshake.
.TP
.B 28
Authentication failed \- usually a wrong password.
.TP
.B 34
The command line was invalid.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B There is no way to trust a private certificate authority.
.B ssl
and
.B wss
connections are verified against the system trust store, so a server whose
certificate is self\-signed, or issued by a CA the system does not already know,
can only be reached with
.BR \-\-ssl\-insecure ,
which skips verification entirely rather than trusting that one certificate. Such
a connection is encrypted but not authenticated, and is therefore open to
interception.
.PP
Under a native Wayland session, the Wayland protocol does not let a client query or
set its absolute position on the desktop, and has no equivalent of X11's
override\-redirect windows. As a result: the server's tooltips, menus and
drop\-downs become undecorated but still managed windows; server\-initiated window
moves apply the size only; the client reports position (0, 0) to the server; and
the NumLock state is not reported. Running under XWayland avoids all of these.
.PP
There is no system tray icon on Linux \- the freedesktop StatusNotifierItem
protocol would need a D\-Bus dependency, and the older XEmbed tray is X11\-only \-
and therefore no desktop notifications either; notifications are logged instead.
.PP
Speaker forwarding (server to client audio) and
.B h264
decoding are implemented for MS Windows only. Microphone forwarding, file
transfers, printing and webcam forwarding are not implemented at all.
